Decide how one new stored claim relates to older accepted claims about the same subject.

A stored claim is a source-supported memory assertion. The incoming claim is newly queued for consolidation. A target
claim is an older accepted claim that was selected as a possible version of the same durable memory.

Compare the proposition in the incoming claim with each available target. Return `no_change` when they describe
compatible values, the same state, additional detail, or independent durable fields. Return `truth_change` when the
incoming and target claims give different incompatible values for the same durable field. A later state can replace
an earlier state even though both were true at different times; that relation is `supersedes`. Use `supersedes` when
the incoming evidence establishes its value as later or as a replacement. Use `contradicts` when incompatible values
have no established ordering. A truth change names the shared durable field, both values, and the evidence
establishing the transition or incompatibility. A target listed under an earlier decision is no longer available.

Every contradiction or replacement requires user review. Recency by itself does not establish a truth change. Prior
review results are authoritative. Do not write display facts or change claim ownership. Return the one schema-valid
decision requested by the output contract.
